<h1>draw</h1>
<p class="sub">A strictly minimal diagramming tool on a precise, programmable grid.</p>

<p>draw is a network and systems diagramming application. Diagrams are laid out on an exact grid
rather than by free positioning, so the same drawing is reproducible and diffable. It is operated by
apnex at <code>draw.apnex.io</code>.</p>

<h2>What it does</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Draw on a grid.</strong> Nodes, links, waypoints, zones and groups snap to a
      centre-origin grid, with routed multi-segment connections.</li>
  <li><strong>Diagrams as interactive UI.</strong> Regions of a diagram can carry actions and
      editable inputs — the application renders its own control bar as a diagram.</li>
  <li><strong>Drivable by a human or an agent.</strong> The document is held by the server and can
      be read and mutated over a REST API or a websocket, with full undo history, so a person and a
      program can work against the same diagram.</li>
  <li><strong>Export.</strong> Any diagram can be served as a self-contained SVG.</li>
  <li><strong>Push to Google Slides.</strong> Optionally, a diagram can be pushed into a Google
      Slides presentation you nominate. This is opt-in and requires you to grant access.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Source</h2>
<p>draw is open source. The full source, including the server, the client and the geometry kernel,
is at <a href="https://github.com/apnex/drawv2">github.com/apnex/drawv2</a>.</p>

<h2>Access</h2>
<p>The application itself is private and requires sign-in with Google. This page, the privacy policy
and the terms are public so that they can be read without an account.</p>

